# EIGENROOM — What a Room Keeps
# play order. one piece per line. the record is itself a power iteration:
# it opens with one knock in an A-major room and ends with the same knock heard
# through that room sixty-four times.
#
# side one: rooms built from math, rooms built from learning
510-room-tone.sh           # the room, introduced: corner, wall, centre
501-power-iteration.sh     # the manual reads itself into the room until the room's chord is left
503-floor-plan.sh          # a drum kit made of four chord-shaped rooms
502-overtone-lessons.sh    # a song you never hear, sung back in a drone's overtones
504-libavfilter-choir.sh   # the library these filters live in, read aloud and sung in A
#
# side two: listening machines, and the way out
506-coincidence.sh         # the upmixer hears only what two voices share
508-terzo-suono.sh         # the correlator hears only their difference: a bass nobody plays
509-above-and-below.sh     # both reports at once; the duet itself is missing
505-tesseract.sh           # a room that cannot be built, walked through
511-eigenvector.sh         # the opening knock, through the room 1, 2, 4 ... 64 times
#
# outtake: 507-dead-air.sh — rhythm decided by how long a room rings. the idea is right,
# the take is too dark and too even. left in pieces/ for whoever wants to finish it.
